                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/cohomology+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Details"} Details
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_shifted_cfield_flux"} Relation to shifted C-field flux
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The effective bosonic exponentiated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superstring"} type II superstring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+strings"} open strings
            ending on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            has three factors:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+holonomy"} higher holonomy
                of the background
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
                over the string 2-dimensional worldsheet;
            %li
              %p
                the ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chan-Paton+bundle"} Chan-Paton bundle
                on the D-brane along the boundary of the string;
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berezin+integral"} Berezinian
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
                over the fermions.
          %p
            Each single contribution is in general not a globally well defined function on the space of string configurations, instead each is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of a possibly non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            over the configuration space (the last one for instance of the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pfaffian+bundle"} Pfaffian bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            of these three line bundles.
          %p
            The non-triviality of this tensor product line bundle (as a line bundle
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                Freed-Witten-Kapustin
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
            = succeed "." do
              %em Freed-Witten anomaly cancellation condition
          %p
            More precisely, the naive holonomy of an ordinary Chan-Paton
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
            would be globally well defined. But in order to cancel the anomaly contribution from the other two factors, one may take the Chan-Paton bundle to be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+bundle"} twisted bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            restricted to the brane. Then its holonomy becomes anomalous, too, but there are then interesting configurations where the product of all three anomalies cancels. This refined argument has been made precise by Kapustin, and so one should probably speak of the
            = succeed "." do
              %em Freed-Witten-Kapustin anomaly cancellation
          %h2#Details Details
          %p
            We interpret the Freed-Witten-Kapustin mechanism in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/push-forward+in+generalized+cohomology"} push-forward in generalized cohomology
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            interpreted in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            with push-forward maps given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+morphisms"} dual morphisms
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality+C%2A-algebras"} Poincaré duality C*-algebras
            (based on
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BrodzkiMathaiRosenbergSzabo06"} Brodzki-Mathai-Rosenberg-Szabo 06, section 7
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Tu06"} Tu 06
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Q
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon Q \to X
            be a map of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi \colon X \to B^2 U(1)
            modulate a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+2-bundle"} circle 2-bundle
            regarded as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twist for K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+groupoid+convolution+algebras"} twisted groupoid convolution algebras
            yields a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            morphism of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi i
                        %mo *
                      %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟵
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi i
                        %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C_{i^\ast \chi}(Q)
                  \\stackrel{i^\ast}{\longleftarrow}
                  C_{\chi}(X)
                  \\,,
          %p
            with notation as in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "Poincar&#233;+duality+algebra#CStarAlgebraOf2BundleOnManifold"} this definition
            %a{:href => "Poincar&#233;+duality+algebra#DualOfCompactManifoldWithTwist"} this proposition
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+morphism"} dual morphism
            is of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi i
                          %mo *
                        %mi χ
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msub
                          %mi W
                          %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi T
                        %mi Q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mo !
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi χ
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msub
                          %mi W
                          %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi T
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C_{\frac{1}{i^\ast \chi \otimes W_3(T Q)}}(Q)
                  \\stackrel{i_!}{\longrightarrow}
                  C_{\frac{1}{\chi \otimes W_3(T X)}}(X)
                  \\,.
          %p
            If we redefine the twist on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to absorb this “quantum correction” as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi χ
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi \mapsto \frac{1}{\chi \otimes W_3(T X)}
            then this is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi i
                        %mo *
                      %mi χ
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi W
                            %mn 3
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi i
                            %mo *
                          %mi T
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi W
                            %mn 3
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi T
                          %mi Q
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mo !
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C_{i^\ast \chi\frac{W_3(i^\ast T X)}{W_3(T Q)}}(Q)
                  \\stackrel{i_!}{\longrightarrow}
                  C_{\chi}(X)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where now we may interpret
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi i
                        %mo *
                      %msub
                        %mi τ
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi τ
                        %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{W_3(i^\ast \tau_X)}{W_3(\tau_Q)}
            as the third
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+Stiefel-Whitney+class"} integral Stiefel-Whitney class
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+bundle"} normal bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N Q
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Nuiten"} Nuiten
          %p
            Postcomposition with this map in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            now yields a map from the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mi χ
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi W
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^\ast \chi \otimes W_3(N Q)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted K-theory
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            to the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted K-theory
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi N
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mi i
                        %mo *
                      %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  i_! \colon K_{\bullet + W_3(N Q) + i^\ast \chi}(Q) \to K_{\bullet +\chi}(X)
                  \\,.
          %p
            If we here think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon Q \hookrightarrow X
            as being the inclusion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi
            would be the class of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            and an element
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi N
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mi i
                        %mo *
                      %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\xi] \in K_{\bullet + W_3(N Q) + i^\ast \chi}(Q)
          %p
            is called (the K-class of) a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chan-Paton+gauge+field"} Chan-Paton gauge field
            on the D-brane satisfying the
            %strong Freed-Witten-Kapustin anomaly cancellation
            mechanism. (The orginal
            %strong Freed-Witten anomaly cancellation
            assumes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ξ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi
            given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+unitary+bundle"} twisted line bundle
            in which case it exhibits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+spin%5Ec+structure"} twisted spin^c structure
            on
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration"} push-forward
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo !
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [i_! \xi] \in K_{\bullet + \chi}(X)
          %p
            is called the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charge"} D-brane charge
